@startuml
!include theme.iuml
title Option desk - Seventeen structures, tagged by the directional view each needs

rectangle "-2\nstrong bearish" as sb
rectangle "-1\nmild bearish" as mb
rectangle "0\nneutral" as n #C6E9C6
rectangle "+1\nmild bullish" as mu
rectangle "+2\nstrong bullish" as su

sb -right- mb
mb -right- n
n -right- mu
mu -right- su

rectangle "long_put\nprotective_put" as lp
rectangle "bear_put_spread" as bps
rectangle "iron_condor, iron_butterfly\nlong_call_butterfly\ncash_secured_put, covered_call*" as ic
rectangle "bull_call_spread\ncovered_call" as bcs
rectangle "long_call\nstraddle, strangle**" as lc

sb .down.> lp
mb .down.> bps
n .down.> ic
mu .down.> bcs
su .down.> lc

rectangle "Asymmetric by design:\nratio_spread (uncapped short side)\nbroken_wing_butterfly (one-sided risk,\noften a credit)\njade_lizard (no upside risk only when\ncredit > call-spread width, and it reports\nwhether that actually holds)" as asym
rectangle "Two expiries, from a pair of snapshots:\ncalendar_spread, diagonal_spread\n(--far-snapshot, or the next expiry\nfound on disk)" as timesp

note bottom of n
Three of the five outlooks sit inside the
one-standard-deviation expected move and
two are extreme: a spread reaches maximum
profit on a normal move, a naked long option
needs an extreme one. --recommend ranks
against these tags.
end note

note right of lc
** straddle and strangle are direction-agnostic
volatility trades listed under the extreme
buckets because they need a large move.
* covered_call spans neutral to mild bullish.
end note

caption Builders return legs; the payoff engine, comparison, backtest and forward test pick every new structure up automatically.
@enduml
